               <persName role="dedicatee" xml:id="persName_6567e2b7">Viggo de Neergaard</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_a37473ee">
               <date isodate="1882-06-17" xml:id="date_7f7b234f">1882-06-17</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_f42c0219">
               <p xml:id="idm72">In the spring of 1882 Hartmann visited his relative Viggo de Neergaard (1837-1915) at his estate Fuglsang on the island of Lolland. In a letter dated Fuglsang 3 June 1882 he wrote a.o.: "That Wednesday everything in the nature seriously began to accelerate; Sofus [Zinn] and I walked in the empty garden seeing wistfully how the flowering bushes had started unfolding and how everything began to be exactly the way we had hoped it would be during your stay; and in the evening the swan surprised me with five neat cubs, - I think it would have pleased you to see this sudden change." The piano piece was Hartmann's responce to Neergaard's letter.</p>
            </history>
